  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Rectangular Form of a Phasor Sum",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the rectangular-form phasor sum for same-frequency cosines by resolving each phasor into real and imaginary components and adding corresponding components.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Rectangular Form of a Phasor Sum",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Rectangular Form of a Phasor Sum",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the earlier phasor representation, keep the common $\\omega$ fixed and rewrite each same-frequency phasor in rectangular form. A rectangular phasor has the form $a+jb$, where $a$ is the real part and $b$ is the imaginary part.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X = A\\cos\\phi + jA\\sin\\phi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So if $X_1 = a_1 + jb_1$ and $X_2 = a_2 + jb_2$, the phasor sum is formed componentwise: add the real parts together and add the imaginary parts together.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_1 + X_2 = (a_1 + a_2) + j(b_1 + b_2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same bookkeeping works for three or more phasors. Collect every real part into one total and every imaginary part into another total, and keep the result rectangular for now.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sum_{k=1}^{n}(a_k + jb_k) = \\left(\\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k\\right) + j\\left(\\sum_{k=1}^{n} b_k\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the rule the later examples will reuse: same $\\omega$, rectangular components, and componentwise addition. The next step can turn this rectangular sum into polar form, but this section stops at the rectangular result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Rectangular Form of a Phasor Sum",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Adding Axis-Aligned Phasor Components",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Add the phasors $3 + j0$ and $0 + j5$ and write the result in rectangular form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "These phasors are already axis-aligned, so the rectangular parts are visible immediately. Write them as $X_1 = 3 + j0$ and $X_2 = 0 + j5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nX_1 &= 3 + j0 \\\\\nX_2 &= 0 + j5\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now add the real parts and the imaginary parts separately. The real-part total is $3 + 0$, and the imaginary-part total is $0 + 5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nX_1 + X_2 &= (3 + 0) + j(0 + 5) \\\\\n&= 3 + j5\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the rectangular-form phasor sum is $3 + j5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Rectangular Form of a Phasor Sum",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Resolving Two Cosine Phasors into Rectangular Components",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the rectangular-form phasor sum of $4\\cos(\\omega t + 60^\\circ)$ and $6\\cos(\\omega t - 30^\\circ)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the two cosines share the same $\\omega$, convert each one to a phasor first. Write $X_1 = 4\\angle 60^\\circ$ and $X_2 = 6\\angle -30^\\circ$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nX_1 &= 4\\angle 60^\\circ = 4\\cos 60^\\circ + j4\\sin 60^\\circ \\\\\nX_2 &= 6\\angle -30^\\circ = 6\\cos(-30^\\circ) + j6\\sin(-30^\\circ)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now resolve the real and imaginary parts of each phasor. The first phasor becomes $X_1 = 2 + j2\\sqrt{3}$, and the second becomes $X_2 = 3\\sqrt{3} - j3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nX_1 &= 4\\cos 60^\\circ + j4\\sin 60^\\circ = 2 + j2\\sqrt{3} \\\\\nX_2 &= 6\\cos(-30^\\circ) + j6\\sin(-30^\\circ) = 3\\sqrt{3} - j3 \\\\\nX_1 + X_2 &= (2 + 3\\sqrt{3}) + j(2\\sqrt{3} - 3)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the rectangular-form phasor sum is $(2 + 3\\sqrt{3}) + j(2\\sqrt{3} - 3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Rectangular Form of a Phasor Sum",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Adding Phasors with Negative Rectangular Components",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Add the phasors $(-4 + j3)$ and $(1 - j6)$ and write the result in rectangular form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "These phasors are already in rectangular form, so preserve the signs and add the real parts and imaginary parts separately. Call them $X_1 = -4 + j3$ and $X_2 = 1 - j6$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nX_1 &= -4 + j3 \\\\\nX_2 &= 1 - j6\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now add the signed real parts and the signed imaginary parts. The real-part total is $-4 + 1 = -3$, and the imaginary-part total is $3 + (-6) = -3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nX_1 + X_2 &= (-4 + 1) + j(3 - 6) \\\\\n&= -3 - j3\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the rectangular-form sum is $-3 - j3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Rectangular Form of a Phasor Sum",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Adding More Than Two Same-Frequency Phasors in Rectangular Form",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Add $4 + j0$, $-1 + j3$, and $2 - j2$ and write the result in rectangular form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "All three terms are already in rectangular form, so keep the signs and add the real parts and imaginary parts across the whole list. Label them $X_1 = 4 + j0$, $X_2 = -1 + j3$, and $X_3 = 2 - j2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nX_1 &= 4 + j0 \\\\\nX_2 &= -1 + j3 \\\\\nX_3 &= 2 - j2\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Group the real parts as $4 + (-1) + 2$ and the imaginary parts as $0 + 3 + (-2)$. Then add each group separately.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nX_1 + X_2 + X_3 &= (4 + (-1) + 2) + j(0 + 3 + (-2)) \\\\\n&= 5 + j1\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the rectangular-form sum is $5 + j1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
